00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.820 align:start position:0%
on a cold afternoon in Western Canada a  community comes together to grieve the  loss of a close friend and celebrate her  life dr. Scheck afar Shabana had worked  as an obstetrician in Edmonton often  helping recent immigrants she and her  two teenage daughters both of whom were  studying to become medical professionals  died on board Ukraine International  Airlines flight 752 as they were  returning to Canada from Iran for my  friend dr. superfish she was absolutely  a kind person she was always there for  the community she was always there to  step in when help needed so losing her  it's a big loss for the Persian  community 57 Canadians died when their  plane was shot down by an Iranian  missile shortly after taking off from  Tehran 13 members of Edmonton's Iranian  community died in the crash the largest  single loss of life the city has seen  for decades  Edmonton small tight-knit Iranian  Canadian community has been devastated  in the Persian restaurants and cafes and  on online chat groups it seems everybody  knows everybody else when you're just  hitting the news it's not something that  you say but did this really happen for a  restaurateur like ours that we have seen  these people they just take deep  connection this is like kind of a small  family of this restaurant the part of  the story is with the people that we see  all the time if it's difficult it's very  tough the tragedy struck Edmonton's  academic community particularly hard at  least 10 of the victims were students  staff or alumni here at the University  of Alberta their friends say they were  some of the brightest and the best their  lives and their contributions to society  cut short Peyman parson is mourning the  loss of his friend professor Pedram  Mousavi his wife and their two young  daughters describing them as role models  in the community you can see the outpour  of messages from people all across this  country it's it shows the grave impact  that's such an unfortunate incident has  on not just the Iranian Canadian  community but Canada as a whole  fundraisers to help pay for memorials  and create scholarship  are another way people here are honoring  their friends aiming to make sure that  those who were lost will not be  forgotten  David Mercer al Jazeera Edmonton Canada
 

